  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "Continuous-Time System Notation",
    "lesson_core_move": "Interpret continuous-time system notation such as $x(t)$ -> $y(t)$ and $y(t) = T{x(t)} by identifying the input signal$, system operator, and output signal in an input-output model.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Continuous-Time System Notation",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Continuous-Time System Notation",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A continuous-time system is a rule that transforms one signal into another signal at each time $t$. Read these expressions as mappings: the input signal is written on the inside, and the output signal is written as the result of that mapping.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In arrow form, write $x(t) \\to y(t)$ to mean: start from $x(t)$, apply the system rule, and produce $y(t)$ as output.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t) \\to y(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Operator notation is the same idea in function-style language: $y(t) = T\\{x(t)\\}$. The symbol $T$ is the system operator, and the braces show that $T$ takes $x(t)$ as its input signal and returns $y(t)$ as the output signal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t) = T\\{x(t)\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the base case $y(t)=2x(t)$, identify $x(t)$ as input and $y(t)$ as output, then read the same transformation as arrow notation. The rule is scaling by $2$, so this is the same system written two ways.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t) = 2x(t) = T\\{x(t)\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the next sections, first label what is input, output, and operator in each equation; then you will apply the same reading pattern to shifted and scaled system rules while keeping this notation structure stable.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Continuous-Time System Notation",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Identifying Input and Output in Arrow Notation",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Read this arrow form and identify the input/output: $x(t) \\to y(t)=2x(t-1)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue in this example is the arrow form $x(t) \\to y(t)=2x(t-1)$. In this notation, the symbol on the left is the source signal, and the symbol on the right is the destination signal after the system acts.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t) \\to y(t)=2x(t-1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply step one: locate the left endpoint of the arrow. That endpoint is the input signal. Therefore, the input is $x(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply step two: the right endpoint is the output signal, so the output is $y(t)$. The right side also shows the same output as an explicit expression, $y(t)=2x(t-1)$, which reinforces output-side notation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply step three: report the pair with explicit direction. Input is $x(t)$, output is $y(t)$, and the direction is input to output, written as $x(t) \\to y(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t) \\to y(t), \\quad y(t)=2x(t-1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Continuous-Time System Notation",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Identifying the System Operator in Operator Notation",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $y(t)=T\\{x(t)\\}$ and $T\\{x(t)\\}=x(t-1)$, identify the operator and restate the system map in operator form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start by reading the braces in the map $y(t)=T\\{x(t)\\}$: the expression inside $\\{\\cdot\\}$ is the operator input, so $x(t)$ is the argument passed through the system rule.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Next, identify the rule itself from $T\\{x(t)\\}=x(t-1)$. This is a one-step delay operator, because it replaces every instance of $t$ with $t-1$ inside the input signal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=T\\{x(t)\\}=x(t-1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Finally, keep the input/output roles explicit: input is $x(t)$, output is $y(t)$, and $T$ is the operator that maps that input to the output by delaying it one unit.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Continuous-Time System Notation",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Translating Between Arrow Notation and Operator Notation",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Translate this pair exactly and check equivalence: $x(t) \\to y(t)=\\int_0^t x(\\tau)d\\tau$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the given notation form $x(t) \\to y(t)=\\int_0^t x(\\tau)d\\tau$. The arrow form gives the rule in the same order: input is $x(t)$, output is $y(t)$, and the transformation is the integral expression.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Identify the two conversion targets: the output is written as an operator-evaluated input in $y(t)=T\\{x(t)\\}$, so the operator body must be exactly the same expression attached to $x(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{x(t)\\}=\\int_0^t x(\\tau)d\\tau,\\quad y(t)=T\\{x(t)\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Write the equivalent operator representation as $y(t)=T\\{x(t)\\}=\\int_0^t x(\\tau)d\\tau$, and this can be translated back to arrow notation the same way: $x(t) \\to y(t)=\\int_0^t x(\\tau)d\\tau$. The meaning is unchanged in both forms.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Continuous-Time System Notation",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Interpreting System Notation with Named Signals",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For the operator form $y_u(t)=\\mathcal{H}\\{u(t)\\}$ where $\\mathcal{H}\\{u(t)\\}=u(t)^2+1$, identify input/output/operator and rewrite in arrow notation.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the operator form and parse the named endpoints. The output label is $y_u(t)$, the input signal is the argument $u(t)$ inside $\\mathcal{H}\\{\\cdot\\}$, and the operator body is $u(t)^2+1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Match the operator symbol to the same role used earlier: $\\mathcal{H}$ is the system operator, so keep its mapping exactly as $\\mathcal{H}\\{u(t)\\}=u(t)^2+1$ and do not change the underlying transformation when moving notation styles.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_u(t)=\\mathcal{H}\\{u(t)\\}=u(t)^2+1,\\quad u(t)\\to y_u(t)=u(t)^2+1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The equivalent arrow statement is $u(t)\\to y_u(t)=u(t)^2+1$. This confirms the symbolic roles are unchanged by relabeling: input is still the non-braced signal, output is still the mapped signal, and $\\mathcal{H}$ is the same operator.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
